   "text": {
    "L1": "Multiplication has useful patterns, so we can calculate mentally.",
    "L2": "Multiplying by 100 moves every digit two places left. Thus 36 × 100 is 3,600.",
    "L3": "For 50 × 40, multiply the non-zero parts: 5 × 4 is 20, then add two zeros.",
    "L4": "Doubling one factor and halving the other keeps the product the same. So 25 × 16 becomes 50 × 8, and both products are 400.",
    "L5": "Multiplication does not always make a number bigger. Multiplying by 1 keeps it the same, and multiplying by 0 gives zero.",
    "L6": "For a quick estimate, round both numbers first. Thirty-eight times fifty-two is about forty times fifty, or ₹2,000.",
    "L7": "Now choose the product of 25 and 16.",
    "L8": "The correct product is 400, because doubling and halving preserved the product."
   },
